+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The current cart presentation path dereferences the catalog product for every line, and cart calculation/filtering also assumes that product remains available. Order read population already has snapshot fields on the order line but additionally looks up the live product to build the nested product representation. Product deletion therefore breaks both cart reads and order reads.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Make deleted catalog rows a normal, recoverable state for cart hydration, mutation, calculation, and checkout.
+- Make order reads depend on persisted order-line data for the purchased values.
+- Preserve 404 semantics for active product lookup and add-to-cart.
+- Cover the complete requested endpoint matrix with focused integration/regression tests.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing the meaning of product deletion, soft-delete schema, pricing rules, or order totals.
+- Reconstructing live catalog images/options for a deleted product when those values were not captured on the order line.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Clean stale cart lines at the cart boundary.** When a cart is loaded or prepared for mutation/calculation, identify lines whose product is absent or no longer valid, remove them from persistence, and calculate from the surviving set. This keeps all cart entry points consistent and prevents checkout from seeing stale lines. Keeping null lines and teaching every downstream calculator to ignore them would scatter the invariant across more paths.
+- **Keep order-line scalar snapshots authoritative.** Order response mapping will always populate SKU, name, quantity, unit price, and subtotal from the order line, and will make live-product enrichment optional. This preserves the historical purchase even if catalog lookup returns null. Re-linking orders to a replacement product would risk changing historical presentation.
+- **Translate missing active products to 404.** Product lookup and cart-add flows will retain their existing not-found exception handling, ensuring deleted products cannot be silently recreated in carts while successful lookups for other products remain unchanged.
+- **Test at API and service boundaries.** Add integration coverage for the endpoint contracts and targeted service/populator tests where null-product behavior is easiest to isolate. This protects both HTTP status codes and persisted recalculation/order snapshot behavior.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Existing carts may contain malformed lines with no product reference → treat them as stale and remove them during normalization, while logging enough context for diagnosis.
+- [Risk] Removing stale lines during a read changes persisted cart state → this is intentional cleanup required to make later PUT, multi-add, and checkout safe; keep it transactional with cart loading/mutation.
+- [Risk] Some order responses may expose a nested live product object → return the stable scalar order-line fields and omit only unavailable live enrichment, preserving the endpoint's existing shape as far as possible.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No data migration is required. Deploy the normalization and snapshot-read changes; existing stale cart lines are cleaned lazily on access, while existing order lines continue to use their stored values. Rollback is code rollback, with no destructive database operation introduced.
